The GDP slump will result in around 16 million Indonesians falling below the poverty line this year, according to the research center at the Indonesian Economic and Social Association (IEKI).

IEKI said the previous poverty line in Indonesia, at around 13,289 rupiah (US$925.65) or less per person per month at constant 2019 prices would plunge the economy into a recession and increasing the number of people in poverty.

The statement was quoted by economic think tank Institute for Development of Economics and Finance (Indef) in a recent report.

Indef warned the recovery of the domestic economy would be sluggish in 2021 due to the slowdown in exports after a contraction last year, the rising inflation as economic activity picks up and weak government support for households without incomes.

"The slowing of exports recovery, coupled with a weak domestic demand as a result of continued weakness of government household support, could cause the domestic economy to contract again this year," Indef said.

The Bank of Indonesia (BI) seeks to raise its benchmark interest rate by 25 basis points (bp) to 3.75 percent, along with a slow inflation outlook of 2.86 percent for 2021, Median Consensus from six forecasters in Indef’s survey also showed.

"It is important to note that the professional forecasters are maintaining a very cautious policy outlook, with some scope for cuts,” Indef said.

The central bank is also likely to keep the foreign exchange rate swap rates at 0.75 percent for 3x3, 6x6, and 12x12-month terms, though the Bank estimates market rates of the bond instruments held by the exchange rate swap at around 0.3-0.4 percent.

Last year, BI raised its benchmark interest by an accumulated 150 bps to support the rupiah, which ended the year, however, strengthened 10.4 percent against the greenback from the beginning of the year's low.

There appeared to have been an overwhelming consensus for interest rate hikes in October last year from the BI Board of Governor's meetings and aggressive rate hikes in November although there has been no consensus from the January and February meetings this year.

Indef said there has been great uncertainty about BI policies this year, pointing out to the mixed opinions of Board members for February meeting from the IEKI research center at Stategem Academy.

The research center, Indef said, predicted a 15 bps cut in February because the BI had maintained the rate at 4 percent in 10 consecutive meetings in another stable exchange rate and inflationary expectations. It also mentioned that BI has opted for neutral deposit facility and lending facility rates, which had not changed col1ected interest rates resulting in a low transmission mechanism.

Indef, however, said there might be differing opinions at the March Board of Governor meeting if the inflation outlook is more benign than expected.

GDP growth was revised down further to 3.35 percent for 2020 from 3.72 percent announced by BI in last month, mimacking a 0.42 percent contraction for the fourth quarter, the central bank's February report showed.

Full-year inflation rate came in at 1.85 percent for last year, above the annual target of 1-3 percent range.

GDP estimates are predicted to exceed around 4.5 percent for this year, as announced by the finance ministry. However, Indef said that if the economy follows a Bloomberg consensus forecast for a contraction this year, GDP growth would narrow down to around 1.17 percent from the October 2020 revision.

Bi said it is performing its duty as regulator and supervisor in the financial market under a high-level uncertainty scenario surrounding the performance of the financial market.

After the March meeting, the central bank would host a press conference to address market concerns about business operations and outlook, Indef said.

IEKI Director Iman Santosa, speaking with Indef today, said
